.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt NETCONSOLE 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm netconsole
.Nd kernel log over the network
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Sy netconsole Ns = Ns Oo Cm + Oc Ns Oo Cm r Oc Ns Oo Ar src-port Oc Ns Cm @ Ns Oo Ar src-ip Oc Ns Cm / Ns Oo Ar dev Oc Ns Cm \&, Ns Oo Ar tgt-port Oc Ns Cm @ Ns Ar tgt-ip Ns Cm / Ns Op Ar tgt-macaddr
.Pp
.Sy rach2.netconsole
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The kernel sends the records of its log over the network, so that a
machine with no serial port leaves a record of its boot and of a panic
on another machine.
Every record from the first of the boot is sent, those written before
the network card started among them: the kernel keeps 32767 characters
of log until they are sent and drops the newest records whole when that
store is full.
A card is sent nothing until its link has been up for two seconds,
since frames sent the moment a link comes up can be lost beyond the
card; a kernel that panics sends at once.
The records are sent by calling the card's transmit with interrupts
off, which waits for the card by polling and not for an interrupt, so
the record of a panic goes out too.
What the debug shell prints is not a record and is not sent.
.Ss UDP targets
The boot option
.Sy netconsole=
gives one or more targets, separated by
.Ql \&; ,
in the form Linux's netconsole reads.
Each record goes to each target as one UDP datagram, from
.Ar src-ip
port
.Ar src-port
to
.Ar tgt-ip
port
.Ar tgt-port ,
through the interface
.Ar dev ,
in an Ethernet frame to the station address
.Ar tgt-macaddr .
The defaults are Linux's: source port 6665, target port 6666, the
interface
.Ql eth0 ,
and the broadcast station address, which needs no address resolution.
.Ar dev
is an interface name, or a station address written with colons.
.Ar src-ip
and
.Ar tgt-ip
are both IPv4 or both IPv6.
Without
.Ar src-ip ,
a datagram is sent from the interface's first IPv4 address or, for
IPv6, from its first address whose link-local scope matches
.Ar tgt-ip Ns 's ,
taken when it is sent; until the interface has one, the records wait.
Linux refuses such a target instead.
.Pp
Each datagram holds one record, a line as
.Xr dmesg 1
shows it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<6>[0.083000] net: eth0: IPv6 fe80::5054:ff:fe12:3456/64 added
.Ed
.Pp
A line longer than 1000 characters goes in pieces of 1000.
A leading
.Cm +
sends Linux's extended form instead:
.Ql level,seq,usec,flags;text ,
where
.Ar seq
counts the records sent to the target from 0,
.Ar usec
is the record's time since boot in microseconds,
.Ar flags
is
.Ql -
or
.Ql c
for a piece of a longer line after its first, and each byte of
.Ar text
below a space, from 127 up, and the backslash is written
.Ql \exNN .
An extended message longer than 1000 bytes is sent in pieces, each with
.Ql ,ncfrag=OFFSET/TOTAL
before its
.Ql \&; .
.Cm r
after
.Cm +
puts the kernel's release and a comma before each message.
.Pp
A target that cannot be read, or whose interface does not exist, is
logged and left out; the others are kept.
Of several
.Sy netconsole=
options, the last is read.
GRUB's script reads
.Ql \&;
as the end of a command, so a line with two targets quotes the option.
.Ss Broadcast frames
The boot option
.Sy rach2.netconsole
sends the records below IP, in broadcast Ethernet frames of type
0x88b5, so that it needs no address and works while the address
configuration is wrong.
A frame holds up to 512 characters of log after a 16-byte header: the
magic
.Ql RCH2 ,
a boot identifier, a sequence number, the length and a checksum, all
big-endian.
Only a packet socket receives them, which needs root.
The frames use the first interface.
Both options may be given; each form is sent the whole log.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Send the log of the machine at 192.168.0.106 to port 6666 of
192.168.0.100:
.Dl netconsole=6665@192.168.0.106/,6666@192.168.0.100/
.Pp
Keep it there, as an ordinary user:
.Dl nc -lu 6666 > boot.log
.Pp
Send the extended form to an IPv6 host on the link through
.Ql eth1 :
.Dl netconsole=+@/eth1,@fe80::1/
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
The kernel logs each target it takes as
.Ql netconsole: udp: SRC-PORT@SRC-IP/DEV,TGT-PORT@TGT-IP/TGT-MACADDR ,
then
.Ql netconsole: started: network logging, targets N ,
and a target it leaves out as
.Ql netconsole: refused: cannot read the target at 'TEXT'
or
.Ql netconsole: refused: DEV: no such card .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dmesg 1 ,
.Xr nc 1 ,
.Xr bootlog 4 ,
.Xr console 4
.Sh STANDARDS
The option follows Linux 6.12's
.Pa Documentation/networking/netconsole.rst .
Targets cannot be added or changed while the machine runs, which Linux
does through configfs.
.Sh BUGS
Neither form is acknowledged: a datagram or frame lost on the wire is
lost.
